<article class="env-remark" id="8D8D4F">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/8D8D4F">Remark <span data-tag="8D8D4F">7.2.2.13</span>.</a> The functor $A \mapsto K(A,n)$ preserves finite products. This is clear, since the class of Eilenberg-MacLane objects is stable under finite products (Remark <a href="/tag/68102B" data-tag="68102B">7.2.2.3</a>) and the homotopy inverse functor $\pi $ commutes with finite products (since homotopy groups are constructed using pullback and truncation functors, each of which commutes with finite products). </p>
